**No candidate below yields a defensible improvement over heuristic NFS.** Three narrow research questions survive: one could close a rigorous NFS gap; two concern computing arithmetic signals whose usefulness for factoring remains unresolved.

Throughout, \(N=pq\) with distinct odd primes; fixed exceptional primes are excluded where necessary. Equivalences refer to this promise unless stated otherwise. I treat the repository’s \(L[2/5]\) result as its internally audited claim, consistent with the qualification in [README.md](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/README.md). The barriers are scoped results, not general lower bounds.

References support the underlying mathematics; elementary reductions displayed below are deductions here unless attributed. “No application found” means none located in this survey. ★ marks a connection I found surprising, without asserting novelty.

**1. Langlands program / automorphic forms**

| Connection: object and proposed feature | Prior art; factoring use | Verdict | Where it fails |
|---|---|---|---|
| **L1. A single squarefree Hecke coefficient.** Fix a non-CM elliptic curve \(E/\mathbb Q\), with associated eigenform \(f\). Compute only \(A=a_N(f)=a_pa_q\), perhaps through a compressed trace formula or Galois representation. | Bach–Charles, [*The hardness of computing an eigenform*](https://arxiv.org/abs/0708.1192), 2007. The distinction between one coefficient and a coefficient oracle is already examined in [tracedata](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/tracedata_results.md). | **OPEN-WORTH-A-LOOK** | Bach–Charles’s reduction can query prime-power indices; it does not establish that the single integer \(a_N\) factors \(N\). Conversely, no fast computation of this single coefficient without factoring is known here: prime-index algorithms require the local Frobenius operators. Both computation and extraction remain obligations. |
| **L2. Functoriality supplies the missing determinant.** Obtain \(A=a_N(f)\) and \(B=a_N(\operatorname{Sym}^2f)=a_{N^2}(f)\). Then \(S=A^2+N-B=qa_p^2+pa_q^2\). | Bach–Charles, 2007, [arXiv:0708.1192](https://arxiv.org/abs/0708.1192); the symmetric-square interpretation is made explicit in the repository. Used as an oracle reduction. | **FACTORING-EQUIVALENT** | The roots of \(T^2-ST+NA^2\) are \(qa_p^2\) and \(pa_q^2\); their gcds with \(N\) recover the factors when \(A\ne0\). Thus functoriality identifies exactly which extra datum suffices, but provides no cheaper computation of it. The zero-trace cases need separate treatment. |
| **L3. Trace formulas at level \(N\).** Compute \(\dim S_2(\Gamma_0(N))\), or extract the volume term from an exact trace formula. The index is \(\psi(N)=(p+1)(q+1)=N+p+q+1\). | Stein, [*Modular Forms: A Computational Approach*](https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/079), 2007, dimension formulas. Already covered in [geometry](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/geometry_results.md). | **FACTORING-EQUIVALENT** | For \(p,q>3\), the elliptic corrections each have only two possibilities, so the genus gives a bounded list of candidates for \(p+q\). Standard modular-symbol realizations have dimension proportional to \(N\), while formula-based evaluation uses its divisors. An exact spectral formula does not remove either cost. |
| **L4. Fixed twists as local separators.** For fixed Dirichlet characters \(\chi\), compare \(a_N(f\otimes\chi)\) with \(a_N(f)\). | Standard twisting theory; Stein, [2007](https://doi.org/10.1090/gsm/079). No additional factoring application found. | **AGGREGATE** | The twist contributes precisely \(\chi(N)\): \(a_N(f\otimes\chi)=\chi(N)a_N(f)\). For a fixed collection of characters, these multipliers depend only on \(N\) modulo their combined conductor; the separate values \(\chi(p)\) and \(\chi(q)\) remain unavailable. |

**2. Representation theory**

| Connection: object and proposed feature | Prior art; factoring use | Verdict | Where it fails |
|---|---|---|---|
| **R1. ★ Count irreducible representations instead of group elements.** Let \(G=\mathrm{GL}_2(\mathbb Z/N)\). Its number of complex irreducible representations is \(k(G)=(p^2-1)(q^2-1)\). | Green, [*The characters of the finite general linear groups*](https://doi.org/10.1090/S0002-9947-1955-0072878-2), 1955. No published factoring use of this particular scalar located. | **FACTORING-EQUIVALENT** | Since \(k(G)=(N+1)^2-(p+q)^2\), one exact count immediately gives \(p+q\). The answer has only \(O(\log N)\) bits, but character-table enumeration is enormous and the CRT product formula requires the factors. Compact output does not imply compact computation. |
| **R2. Weil-representation traces.** Evaluate the normalized trace of a fixed unipotent element in the Weil representation over \(\mathbb Z/N\). This is a normalized quadratic Gauss sum. | Gurevich–Hadani–Howe, [*Quadratic reciprocity and the sign of the Gauss sum via the finite Weil representation*](https://arxiv.org/abs/0808.2447), 2008. Already surveyed in `newfamily`. | **AGGREGATE** | For a fixed unit parameter \(a\), the normalized trace is determined by a Jacobi symbol and the usual phase depending on \(N\bmod4\), hence by \(N\) modulo a fixed modulus. Degenerate parameters can reveal a gcd, but that is already a splitting event. The representation does not turn the Jacobi product into its two local signs. |
| **R3. Deligne–Lusztig tori become Lucas factoring groups.** Build \(T_d=\ker(\operatorname{Norm}:(\mathbb Z/N)[\sqrt d]^\times\to(\mathbb Z/N)^\times)\). Locally its order is \(r-(d/r)\). | Deligne–Lusztig, [1976](https://doi.org/10.2307/1971021), supply the representation-theoretic setting. Williams’s [\(p+1\) method](https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-1982-0658227-7), 1982, already exploits the torus. | **KNOWN** | Computing powers in this torus is efficient without factoring, but successful separation depends on smoothness of \(p-1\) or \(p+1\). Character degrees and torus orders merely repackage that dependence. Changing the representation does not improve the relevant smoothness distribution. |

**3. Motives / Hodge theory / periods**

| Connection: object and proposed feature | Prior art; factoring use | Verdict | Where it fails |
|---|---|---|---|
| **H1. Cyclotomic motives have a factor-revealing rank.** Describe \(\mathbb Q(\zeta_N)\) from \(N\), and compute the rank of its zero-dimensional motive or Betti realization: \(\varphi(N)\). | Washington, [*Introduction to Cyclotomic Fields*](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4612-1934-7), 1997. The underlying \(\varphi(N)\) reduction is classical. | **FACTORING-EQUIVALENT** | The rank gives \(p+q=N+1-\varphi(N)\). An explicit cyclotomic basis already has roughly \(N\) elements, while the familiar rank formula uses the prime divisors. Calling the rank a motivic invariant supplies no succinct algorithm for evaluating it. |
| **H2. CM periods and ambiguous ideal classes.** From the quadratic order of discriminant \(D=-N\) or \(-4N\), construct CM periods; seek a period relation identifying a nontrivial ambiguous class. | Chowla–Selberg, [*On Epstein’s Zeta-function*](https://doi.org/10.1515/crll.1967.227.86), 1967; Shanks, [*Class number, a theory of factorization, and genera*](https://doi.org/10.1090/pspum/020/0316385), 1971. Class-group factoring is established. | **REDUCES-TO-M** | The useful object is still an order-two ideal class, whose discovery normally requires class-group relations or an annihilator. Period identities do not identify its position in the class group. Numerical evaluation of a principal period alone does not produce the missing class. |
| **H3. Period recurrences and matrix factorials share a real factoring primitive.** Evaluate polynomial-coefficient transfer products modulo \(N\), as in Picard–Fuchs and Cartier–Manin computations. The scalar specialization computes \(m!\bmod N\), with \(m=\lfloor\sqrt N\rfloor\). | Bostan–Gaudry–Schost, [*Linear Recurrences with Polynomial Coefficients and Application to Integer Factorization and Cartier–Manin Operator*](https://doi.org/10.1137/S0097539704443793), 2007. Explicitly used in factoring. | **KNOWN** | Here \(\gcd(m!,N)\) finds the smaller prime, and fast recurrence evaluation accelerates the product. The generic acceleration is essentially square-root in recurrence length, giving the familiar \(N^{1/4}\) scale before later factoring improvements. Cohomological provenance does not provide logarithmic-time evaluation of arbitrary polynomial-coefficient recurrences. |

**4. Algebraic K-theory**

| Connection: object and proposed feature | Prior art; factoring use | Verdict | Where it fails |
|---|---|---|---|
| **K1. ★ Every fixed odd K-group order factors a semiprime.** For fixed \(j\ge1\), compute \(C_j=\#K_{2j-1}(\mathbb Z/N)=(p^j-1)(q^j-1)\). | Quillen, [*On the Cohomology and K-Theory of the General Linear Groups Over a Finite Field*](https://doi.org/10.2307/1970825), 1972. The case \(j=1\) is \(\varphi(N)\); no factoring use of the higher cases located. | **FACTORING-EQUIVALENT** | From \(C_j\), obtain \(p^j+q^j=N^j+1-C_j\), solve a quadratic for \(p^j,q^j\), and take integer roots. Thus even a scalar higher-K invariant with a short answer contains the complete factorization. Quillen’s computation describes the answer after local decomposition; it is not a factor-free evaluation algorithm. |
| **K2. An explicit \(K_1(\mathbb Z[1/N])\) basis.** Compute generators of \(\mathbb Z[1/N]^\times/\{\pm1\}\) as rational numbers, rather than merely its rank. | Weibel, [*The K-book*](https://sites.math.rutgers.edu/~weibel/Kbook.html), 2013, \(K_1\), units and localization. No new factoring application located. | **FACTORING-EQUIVALENT** | The group is generated by \(p,q\). A full basis must contain a rational unit whose \(p\)- and \(q\)-valuations differ; stripping common powers of \(N\) from its numerator and denominator exposes a proper gcd. The rank alone is two and reveals nothing on the semiprime promise. |
| **K3. Regulators and Lichtenbaum formulas as annihilator factories.** Use a quadratic number ring built from \(N\), and special zeta values or regulators to recover a class-group annihilator. | Lichtenbaum, [*On the values of zeta and L-functions I*](https://doi.org/10.2307/1970792), 1972; Schnorr–Lenstra, [1984](https://pub.math.leidenuniv.nl/~lenstrahw/PUBLICATIONS/1984c/artc.pdf), for class-group factoring. | **REDUCES-TO-M** | These formulas relate torsion orders, regulators and analytic values; they do not independently compute the individual factors in that relation. Once an annihilator is obtained, the splitting mechanism is the existing class-group mechanism. Approximating an analytic product does not automatically supply the required exact integer. |

**5. Arithmetic geometry**

| Connection: object and proposed feature | Prior art; factoring use | Verdict | Where it fails |
|---|---|---|---|
| **G1. Vary an abelian variety to vary the hidden order.** Build curves or Jacobians over \(\mathbb Z/N\), multiply points by smooth exponents, and detect unequal reduction behaviour. | Lenstra, [*Factoring integers with elliptic curves*](https://doi.org/10.2307/1971363), 1987. Already a factoring algorithm. | **KNOWN** | The geometric operation succeeds when one local group order is sufficiently smooth. Higher dimension supplies different orders but also larger groups and more expensive arithmetic. No uniform order distribution improving the NFS exponent follows from the additional geometry. |
| **G2. Rational points on a norm torus.** For \(p,q\equiv1\bmod4\), seek two inequivalent integral points on \(x^2+y^2=N\), rather than count points. | Classical Euler factorization through two-square representations; quadratic-form algorithms in Buchmann–Vollmer, [2007](https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-540-46368-9). The repository’s CM-twist construction already uses this bridge. | **FACTORING-EQUIVALENT** | Two inequivalent representations \(N=a^2+b^2=c^2+d^2\) give a proper factor through Gaussian gcds, or \(\gcd(N,ad-bc)\) after suitable orientation. Producing the second representation is the missing work; one representation does not furnish it. This equivalence is restricted to the split–split promise. |
| **G3. Use abc or S-unit geometry to construct sieve relations.** Find \(B\)-smooth \(u,v\) satisfying \(u+v=kN\), with the height and output rate needed by a faster sieve. | Evertse–Győry, [*Unit Equations in Diophantine Number Theory*](https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9781316160749), 2015. Prior coverage: [abcsunit](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/abcsunit_results.md). | **REDUCES-TO-size–entropy** | abc-type statements bound heights or exceptional solutions; they do not provide the required dense, efficiently enumerable family. Effective S-unit algorithms also require generators and can have prohibitive dependence on the support. The repository’s tested constructor classes retain the \(L[2/3]\) per-output obstruction. |

**6. p-adic analysis / perfectoid spaces / prismatic cohomology**

| Connection: object and proposed feature | Prior art; factoring use | Verdict | Where it fails |
|---|---|---|---|
| **P1. Separate the two components of the \(N\)-adic completion.** Start with \(\varprojlim_k\mathbb Z/N^k\cong\mathbb Z_p\times\mathbb Z_q\), and try to apply tilting or a prism separately to its components. | Bhatt–Scholze, [*Prisms and Prismatic Cohomology*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.08229), 2019/2022. No factoring algorithm found. | **RESTATEMENT** | Prismatic theory fixes a prime; choosing the \(p\)-component already chooses a factor of \(N\). The product completion is definable without factoring, but extracting either component supplies a nontrivial CRT projector. Replacing the prime parameter by composite \(N\) does not give the same theory. |
| **P2. Prime-free big Witt vectors.** Compute ghost coordinates and Frobenius/Verschiebung operations on a bounded truncation \(W_S(\mathbb Z/N)\), hoping to expose incompatible local characteristics. | Borger, [*The basic geometry of Witt vectors I*](https://arxiv.org/abs/0801.1691), 2008/2011. No factoring use found. | **REDUCES-TO-PT** | At bounded indices these are universal polynomial operations over the input ring. Inverting the triangular ghost map requires index integers to be units; a failed inversion gives precisely a gcd split. Enumerating indices until one encounters \(p\) amounts to an enormous search, not a new local invariant. |
| **P3. A genuine componentwise Frobenius.** Choose \(d\) with Jacobi symbol \((d/N)=-1\), and build \(A=(\mathbb Z/N)[t]/(t^2-d)\). Ask for the operator reducing to \(p\)-power and \(q\)-power Frobenius locally. | Schoof, [1985](https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-1985-0777280-6), explains the algorithmic importance of Frobenius. Already covered by Prop. FROB in [nonsmooth](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/nonsmooth_results.md). | **FACTORING-EQUIVALENT** | The algebra splits at exactly one prime, so true Frobenius sends \(t\) to \(ct\), with \(c=1\) at one component and \(c=-1\) at the other. Hence \(\gcd(c-1,N)\) factors \(N\). The computable map \(x\mapsto x^N\) is generally neither this operator nor a ring endomorphism. |

**7. Model theory / o-minimality**

| Connection: object and proposed feature | Prior art; factoring use | Verdict | Where it fails |
|---|---|---|---|
| **O1. Apply Pila–Wilkie to the factor hyperbola.** Construct \(X_N=\{(x,y):xy=N\}\), or a logarithmic reparametrization, and seek its sparse integral points. | Pila–Wilkie, [*The rational points of a definable set*](https://doi.org/10.1215/S0012-7094-06-13336-7), 2006. No factoring application found. | **REDUCES-TO-Pila–Wilkie algebraic-part exclusion** | The hyperbola is entirely algebraic, precisely the part excluded from the transcendental counting estimate. Replacing \(x\) by \(\log x\) makes factor points cease to be rational points in the relevant coordinates. Even a small upper bound would not provide an algorithm locating the points. |
| **O2. Encode divisibility through oscillation.** On the balanced-factor interval, integer solutions of \(\sin(\pi N/x)=0\) identify divisors. Try to express this using restricted analytic functions. | Binyamini–Novikov, [*Wilkie’s conjecture for restricted elementary functions*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.04671), 2016/2017. No factoring application found. | **REDUCES-TO-o-minimal uniform finiteness** | Global sine is not definable in an o-minimal expansion of the real field. On a balanced interval the displayed function has \(\Theta(\sqrt N)\) real zeros, so restricted-sine descriptions require growing oscillatory complexity. The short written formula hides that complexity outside the tame setting. |
| **O3. A fixed formula with a hard witness.** In \(\mathbb Z/N\), ask for \(e^2=e\), \(e\ne0,1\). The formula has constant size and quantifier complexity. | Standard idempotent decomposition; Weibel, [2013](https://sites.math.rutgers.edu/~weibel/Kbook.html). The computational interpretation is elementary. | **FACTORING-EQUIVALENT** | A witness immediately gives \(\gcd(e,N)\), and factors construct a witness by CRT. Deciding existence is trivial on the semiprime promise, whereas finding a witness is factoring. Low logical complexity therefore does not imply low bit complexity of witness extraction. |
| **O4. Ax–Kochen transfer at auxiliary primes.** Evaluate bounded-precision local formulas involving \(N\) at a fixed finite set of known primes \(\ell\). | Ax–Kochen, [*Diophantine problems over local fields I*](https://doi.org/10.2307/2373065), 1965. No factoring use found. | **AGGREGATE** | With fixed precisions, the answers factor through \(N\bmod\prod_\ell\ell^{k_\ell}\). Full local-field truth is not automatically a uniformly bounded-precision test near singular fibres. Applying the theory instead at the unknown primes dividing \(N\) requires naming those primes first. |

**8. Additive combinatorics**

| Connection: object and proposed feature | Prior art; factoring use | Verdict | Where it fails |
|---|---|---|---|
| **C1. Constructive sum–product over the composite ring.** Grow sets using addition and multiplication in \(\mathbb Z/N\); hope failure of expansion exposes a proper ideal. | Bourgain–Katz–Tao, [*A sum-product estimate in finite fields, and applications*](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0301343), 2003/2004. No faster factoring construction located. | **REDUCES-TO-PT** | A constructive theorem returning a generator of a proper ideal would indeed factor \(N\). Existing field growth estimates provide neither that generator nor a short procedure reaching the obstruction; ring-arithmetic implementations must still encounter a nonunit or unequal local collision. PT applies to that restricted implementation, not to every possible use of integer representatives. |
| **C2. Freiman structure on smooth products.** Take exponent boxes of small primes, use their additive structure in logarithmic coordinates, and seek products in short intervals around \(kN\). | Green–Ruzsa, [*Freiman’s theorem in an arbitrary abelian group*](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0505198), 2005/2007. Related lattice approach already audited in `siconstruct`. | **REDUCES-TO-smooth-construction barrier** | Small doubling of exponent vectors does not imply useful additive structure of their residues or integer values. Locating the required short interval becomes the near-exact prime-lattice approximation problem identified in the repository. A structural description supplies no output-sensitive navigation algorithm through that lattice. |
| **C3. CLP/slice rank to force square products.** Put smooth-number exponent vectors in \((\mathbb Z/4)^r\), and force progressions or other low-degree configurations. | Croot–Lev–Pach, [2016/2017](https://arxiv.org/abs/1605.01506); compare Balister–Bollobás–Morris, [*The sharp threshold for making squares*](https://doi.org/10.4007/annals.2018.188.1.2), 2018. Square-product combinatorics already informs factoring. | **REDUCES-TO-size–entropy** | For rational smooth integers, \(r+1\) parity vectors already guarantee a linear dependency. CLP-type progression thresholds do not improve that elementary fact, and still require obtaining the smooth inputs. Neither slice rank nor parity rank guarantees a nontrivial CRT sign after square-root extraction. |
| **C4. A quadratic refinement of the relation matrix.** View NFS fruitfulness as a hidden linear functional on true square dependencies, arising from mod-4/Bockstein information; use an inverse theorem to prove it cannot lie in the existing row space. | Lee–Venkatesan, [*Rigorous Analysis of a Randomised Number Field Sieve*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1805.08873), 2018. The precise formulation is already developed in [nfsfruit](/Volumes/git/factoring-connection/notes/nfsfruit_results.md). | **OPEN-WORTH-A-LOOK** | Ordinary smoothness estimates and mod-2 rank do not control this extra functional. The required assertion is anti-concentration after simultaneous smoothness conditioning, including unit square classes. It could improve the rigorous exponent to \(1/3\), but would not itself beat heuristic NFS. |

**9. Ergodic theory / homogeneous dynamics**

| Connection: object and proposed feature | Prior art; factoring use | Verdict | Where it fails |
|---|---|---|---|
| **D1. Count a Hecke orbit without enumerating it.** Start with a fixed lattice or elliptic curve and its cyclic degree-\(N\) Hecke correspondence. For squarefree \(N\), its degree is \(\psi(N)=N+p+q+1\). | Clozel–Oh–Ullmo, [*Hecke operators and equidistribution of Hecke points*](https://doi.org/10.1007/s002220100126), 2001. The degree reduction is elementary; related geometry already covered. | **FACTORING-EQUIVALENT** | The exact orbit size immediately gives the factors. Equidistribution concerns normalized averages and does not cheaply recover this normalization. Explicit orbit construction has roughly \(N\) points, while the short degree formula uses the divisors of \(N\). |
| **D2. Duke equidistribution to locate ambiguous CM points.** Walk through CM points or closed geodesics attached to a quadratic discriminant built from \(N\), seeking an order-two ideal class. | Duke, [*Hyperbolic distribution problems and half-integral weight Maass forms*](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF01393993), 1988; Shanks, [1971](https://doi.org/10.1090/pspum/020/0316385). | **REDUCES-TO-M** | Equidistribution of the full packet does not give a fast hitting bound for its few ambiguous classes. Generating and recognizing these classes still uses the class group and its relations or orders. Mixing of ordinary observables does not make this exceptional target dense. |
| **D3. Littlewood-type simultaneous approximation as factor localization.** Build lattices from rational coordinates such as \(a/N,b/N\), and seek short vectors whose coordinates have a proper gcd with \(N\). | Einsiedler–Katok–Lindenstrauss, [*Invariant measures and the set of exceptions to Littlewood’s conjecture*](https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0612721), 2006. Related continued-fraction attempts are already covered in `classE`. | **REDUCES-TO-A** | Rational coordinates already have the public common denominator \(N\); their obvious returns do not isolate \(p\). A useful return must satisfy an additional divisibility condition that approximation alone does not enforce. Measure rigidity provides neither that condition nor the required uniform, constructive hitting-time bound. |

**10. Galois cohomology / class field theory**

| Connection: object and proposed feature | Prior art; factoring use | Verdict | Where it fails |
|---|---|---|---|
| **S1. Fixed abelian Artin symbols.** Evaluate the Artin symbol of the ideal \((N)\) in a fixed abelian extension, hoping to separate its prime-ideal contributions. | Classical reciprocity; Borger–de Smit, [*Explicit class field theory and the algebraic geometry of \(\Lambda\)-rings*](https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.02295), 2018. Already covered by the repository’s reciprocity theorem. | **AGGREGATE** | Reciprocity computes the product of the local symbols from a fixed ray-class residue. Restricting rational integers to that residue gives a function of \(N\bmod m\). Recovering either individual local symbol is an additional problem, not an output of reciprocity. |
| **S2. Selmer matrices for an \(N\)-dependent curve.** Build \(E_N:y^2=x(x-N)(x+N)\); compute a descent matrix at its bad places and hope its kernel distinguishes \(p,q\). | Poonen–Schaefer, [*Explicit descent for Jacobians of cyclic covers*](https://doi.org/10.1515/crll.1997.488.141), 1997; Poonen–Rains, [2012](https://arxiv.org/abs/1009.0287), for Selmer distributions. | **RESTATEMENT** | Standard explicit descent first constructs square-class groups supported at the bad primes and imposes conditions at those individual primes. For this curve that preprocessing requires the prime divisors of \(N\). A scalar Selmer rank could be weaker than factoring, but the proposed local-matrix algorithm has already used the factors. |
| **S3. Trivialize a Brauer class constructively.** Replace relation collection by a uniform algorithm finding zero divisors or explicit matrix-algebra isomorphisms for promised split quaternion algebras supplied by an \(N\)-only reduction. | Rónyai, [*Simple algebras are difficult*](https://doi.org/10.1145/28395.28438), 1987, and [*Zero divisors in quaternion algebras*](https://doi.org/10.1016/0196-6774(88)90014-4), 1988; Cremona–Rusin, [2003](https://doi.org/10.1090/S0025-5718-02-01480-1). Known oracle reductions. | **FACTORING-EQUIVALENT** | The relevant uniform explicit-splitting problem already admits factoring reductions; factoring oracles also support polynomial-time conic-solving methods. Knowing that the Brauer class vanishes does not supply a small constructive trivialization. This statement concerns the general primitive, not every fixed quaternion family. |
| **S4. Cohen–Lenstra as a guide to favourable class groups.** Vary discriminants containing \(N\), hoping unusually smooth class numbers or small 2-primary obstructions produce rapid splitting. | Cohen–Lenstra, [1984](https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0099440); Schnorr–Lenstra, [*A Monte Carlo factoring algorithm with linear storage*](https://pub.math.leidenuniv.nl/~lenstrahw/PUBLICATIONS/1984c/artc.pdf), 1984. Already used in factoring. | **KNOWN** | Distributional heuristics do not identify favourable discriminants cheaply or guarantee them for every input. The successful event remains smoothness of a class-group order followed by extraction of an ambiguous class. Small Selmer or class-group rank alone does not provide the necessary elements. |
| **S5. ★ Fixed nonabelian Artin data through a weight-one form.** Let \(F=\mathbb Q(\alpha)\), \(\alpha^3-\alpha-1=0\), and \(b_n=[q^n]\eta(z)\eta(23z)\). At unramified primes, \(b_r\in\{2,0,-1\}\) records the three \(S_3\) Frobenius classes; investigate factor-free computation at \(N\). | Zagier, [*Elliptic Modular Forms and Their Applications*](https://www.its.caltech.edu/~matilde/Zagier123ModularForms.pdf), 2008, §3, explains this exact correspondence. Fixed-cubic root counts are already an opening in `tracedata`; no faster factoring application found. | **OPEN-WORTH-A-LOOK** | This signal is not determined by a fixed collection of abelian congruence symbols. However, fast composite-index coefficient extraction is missing, and a bounded output is not known to factor general \(N\). Weight one also removes the factor-sized determinant that makes the Bach–Charles extraction work in higher weight. |

**Ranked shortlist**

**1. C4 — Prove fruitfulness using the quadratic refinement of NFS relations.**

This has the clearest algorithmic consequence: a suitable theorem would turn the rigorous relation-generation bound into rigorous \(L[1/3]\) factoring, improving the project’s \(2/5\) exponent.

Write \(x(z)\in\mathbb F_2^C\) for the complete square-class features of a kept relation, and \(\beta(z)\) for its Bockstein refinement. The repository identifies the sufficient quantity
\[
\delta=\min_w\Pr\!\left[\beta(z)\ne\langle w,x(z)\rangle\right].
\]
A bound \(\delta\ge L_N[1/3,-o(1)]\), on a sufficiently frequent set of sampled polynomial parameters, would allow batch enlargement without changing the leading complexity.

**First-week question:** for an irreducible cubic field, can \(\beta\), modulo existing rows, be expressed using ideal valuations modulo four and at most two unit characters, **without constructing a full S-unit basis**? Derive that expression with uniform bounds, or identify the exact class-group obstruction. This tests whether available character-sum machinery can even address the missing statistic.

This is an existing repository opening; the proposed contribution is a concrete bridge to quadratic-refinement and inverse-theorem techniques.

**2. L1 — Separate single-coefficient extraction from coefficient-oracle hardness.**

The narrow question remains meaningful because neither symmetric-square data nor fixed twists answer it.

**First-week question:** fix a non-CM curve, for example \(37\mathrm{a}1\). Given
\[
N,\qquad A=a_pa_q\ne0,
\]
and additionally the complete factorization of \(A\), can one extract a factor of \(N\) efficiently on any explicitly defined positive-density family? Start by enumerating possible trace divisors and determining whether their Hasse bounds and known Galois constraints yield a usable small-root equation.

The factorization of \(A\) is an auxiliary resource whose cost must eventually be charged. A successful extraction lemma would still leave the computation of \(A\) unresolved; a negative result should state precisely which algebraic or congruence-based extraction class it excludes.

**3. S5 — Determine the computational strength of fixed nonabelian weight-one coefficients.**

This is the weakest algorithmic prospect, but a useful boundary test. The observable lies beyond fixed abelian residue data while avoiding the higher-weight determinant reduction.

**First-week question:** starting from
\[
\eta(z)\eta(23z)=\tfrac12\bigl(\Theta_{x^2+xy+6y^2}
-\Theta_{2x^2+xy+3y^2}\bigr),
\]
derive a complete factor-free specification for computing \(b_N\bmod2\), and audit the fastest quadratic-form route: exactly which modular square roots or representation searches does it require? Determine whether those calls can be eliminated within that route, rather than hidden in a coefficient oracle.

A useful check is that all \(b_{N^j}\) depend only on the unordered pair of local \(S_3\) classes. Thus prime-power queries reveal splitting types but introduce no factor-sized determinant. Any successful extraction must use something beyond the higher-weight argument.

The surprising negative reductions are **R1** and **K1**: tiny representation-theoretic and K-theoretic answers determine the factors exactly. I found no published factoring use of those particular formulations, but they are elementary consequences of classical formulas. None of the three shortlisted questions currently supplies a fourth efficiently computable leak family or a route below heuristic \(L[1/3]\).